1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Where may a card number live in your system?

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:06,048
In Lesson 1, the card zone was the only place a card number ever lives.

3
00:00:06,098 --> 00:00:12,168
In Lesson 3, a card number was shown masked, kept as a token, and never copied around.

4
00:00:12,218 --> 00:00:18,312
This lesson explains why: the card industry's rulebook, and how one locked room keeps it small.

5
00:00:18,362 --> 00:00:23,568
Picture a shop that keeps card numbers in its orders table, just in case it needs them for refunds.

6
00:00:23,618 --> 00:00:25,440
It seems harmless.

7
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,312
Then the numbers spread.

8
00:00:27,362 --> 00:00:30,216
A report reads the table every morning.

9
00:00:30,266 --> 00:00:32,448
A backup copies it every night.

10
00:00:32,498 --> 00:00:34,920
Every developer's laptop can reach it.

11
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:39,552
Now each of those places holds card numbers too, so each of them must be checked.

12
00:00:39,602 --> 00:00:43,416
One weak laptop can leak every card the shop has ever seen.

13
00:00:43,466 --> 00:00:45,384
Those checks come from one rulebook.

14
00:00:45,434 --> 00:00:49,464
PCI DSS is the card industry's security rulebook.

15
00:00:49,514 --> 00:00:53,760
It checks every system that stores, handles or sends a card number.

16
00:00:53,810 --> 00:00:58,944
It also checks every system connected to those, or able to change how safe they are.

17
00:00:58,994 --> 00:01:03,936
The check covers the system's network, its logins, its logs and its changes.

18
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:09,432
So in the shop's story, the report, the backup and every laptop are now in the check.

19
00:01:09,482 --> 00:01:14,496
The more places a card number can reach, the bigger the check, and the more ways to fail it.

20
00:01:14,546 --> 00:01:17,448
So the way out is to have less to check.

21
00:01:17,498 --> 00:01:19,368
Think of a coat check at a theatre.

22
00:01:19,418 --> 00:01:21,360
Coats go into one locked room.

23
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:23,232
Everyone else gets a ticket.

24
00:01:23,282 --> 00:01:27,408
The ticket brings your coat back, but a thief who steals it gets no coat.

25
00:01:27,458 --> 00:01:29,280
Card numbers work the same way.

26
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:35,568
They go into one locked room, and every other system gets a ticket: the card token, never the number.

27
00:01:35,618 --> 00:01:38,904
In this system, the locked room is the card zone.

28
00:01:38,954 --> 00:01:42,960
It has its own database, its own keys and its own logins.

29
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:44,952
It shares none of them with ecom.

30
00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:46,824
It has only two doors.

31
00:01:46,874 --> 00:01:50,208
One door is for buyers' browsers: the card form.

32
00:01:50,258 --> 00:01:53,760
The other door is for partner systems, like ecom.

33
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:56,448
The database inside has no door at all.

34
00:01:56,498 --> 00:01:58,320
Now watch the product.

35
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:02,400
This room has no screen of its own, so watch how the system behaves.

36
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:04,824
A buyer opens the bank's payment page.

37
00:02:04,874 --> 00:02:09,864
The card form under "Card details" is the first door, opened inside the page.

38
00:02:09,914 --> 00:02:14,016
The buyer types the card, and it travels straight through that door into the room.

39
00:02:14,066 --> 00:02:15,960
ecom's servers are not on the way.

40
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:20,760
Inside the room, the card is locked away, and a token comes back out.

41
00:02:20,810 --> 00:02:27,288
Then ecom knocks on the second door: it asks the room to charge the card, and it names the card only by its token.

42
00:02:27,338 --> 00:02:29,472
Now look at the running card zone itself.

43
00:02:29,522 --> 00:02:31,368
Two doors answer from outside.

44
00:02:31,418 --> 00:02:34,488
The database answers to nobody outside the room.

45
00:02:34,538 --> 00:02:39,048
And in the bank admin portal, every payment shows only a masked number.

46
00:02:39,098 --> 00:02:41,664
ecom holds tickets, never coats.

47
00:02:41,714 --> 00:02:43,536
Now the honest part.

48
00:02:43,586 --> 00:02:45,408
ecom still gets a check.

49
00:02:45,458 --> 00:02:49,896
It shows the card form on its page, and it uses tickets to take payments.

50
00:02:49,946 --> 00:02:53,952
So it is connected to the room, and its check is smaller, but not none.

51
00:02:54,002 --> 00:02:55,992
And the room needs a building.

52
00:02:56,042 --> 00:03:00,528
In a bank, the card zone runs inside the bank's own secured network.

53
00:03:00,578 --> 00:03:04,128
The bank's firewalls, guards and monitoring stand around it.

54
00:03:04,178 --> 00:03:12,288
The card zone fits into that network through its two narrow doors, and it shares no parts with the bank's other systems, so it breaks nothing around it.

55
00:03:12,338 --> 00:03:15,216
The product claims no certificate for itself.

56
00:03:15,266 --> 00:03:18,696
The bank's own assessor checks the room and the building together.

57
00:03:18,746 --> 00:03:20,568
What does this protect?

58
00:03:20,618 --> 00:03:27,456
Without the room, every report, backup and laptop holds card numbers, and any one of them can leak them all.

59
00:03:27,506 --> 00:03:31,248
With the room, a leak from ecom leaks no card number at all.

60
00:03:31,298 --> 00:03:38,064
The buyer's card stays safe, ecom's check stays small, and the bank guards one small room instead of everything.

61
00:03:38,114 --> 00:03:39,936
Back to the coat check.

62
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:43,080
Coats in one room, tickets everywhere else.

63
00:03:43,130 --> 00:03:46,392
We deliver the locked room; the bank owns the building.

64
00:03:46,442 --> 00:03:50,304
Next: how ecom takes a payment without ever seeing the card.
